    <code code="10154-3" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Chief complaint Narrative - Reported"/>
    <title>Chief Complaint</title>
    <text><paragraph>Low back pain since lifting bags of clay at the ball field two days ago. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mr. Thorpe called the clinic and was seen the same day. Two days ago, while preparing the infield for the young players he coaches, he lifted and carried a dozen bags of infield clay and felt his lower back tighten that evening. The pain is across the low back, worse on bending and getting out of a chair, and eases when he lies on his side or walks slowly. It does not go into his legs. He has no numbness, weakness, trouble passing urine or controlling his bowels, fever, weight loss, or night pain. He took two ibuprofen tablets yesterday before remembering that he was told to avoid them with his heart condition and aspirin. He has had no chest tightness and has not needed nitroglycerin; he skipped yesterday&apos;s cardiac rehabilitation session because of the pain. His lip has healed since the surgery eight weeks ago. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Onset</td><td>Two days ago, after lifting and carrying bags of clay</td></tr><tr><td>Location</td><td>Across the lower back, more on the right; no leg pain</td></tr><tr><td>Duration</td><td>Two days</td></tr><tr><td>Character</td><td>Tight, aching, with catches on bending</td></tr><tr><td>Severity</td><td>6 of 10 on bending; 3 of 10 at rest</td></tr><tr><td>Timing</td><td>Worse in the morning and on rising from a chair</td></tr><tr><td>Context</td><td>Coronary disease on aspirin, atorvastatin, ezetimibe and metoprolol; in cardiac rehabilitation; lip cancer excised eight weeks ago</td></tr><tr><td>Modifying factors</td><td>Better lying on his side and walking slowly; two ibuprofen tablets once</td></tr><tr><td>Associated signs and symptoms</td><td>No numbness, weakness, bladder or bowel change, fever, weight loss, night pain, or chest pain</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

