<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Acute low back strain (S39.012A)</content>. Mechanical low back strain from heavy lifting, with a normal neurological examination. His only red flag is a history of cancer, but an early lip cancer excised with clear margins eight weeks ago very rarely spreads to bone, the pain began with lifting, and there is no midline tenderness, night pain or weight loss, so imaging is not needed now. Anti-inflammatory medicines are avoided because they raise the risk of heart attack in coronary disease and ibuprofen can block the effect of aspirin; muscle relaxants and opioids are avoided because they sedate and rarely help. [S]<list><item>Acetaminophen 1,000 mg every 8 hours as needed, no more than 3,000 mg a day.</item><item>Heat, short walks, and normal activity as tolerated; no bed rest.</item><item>No ibuprofen or other anti-inflammatory medicines.</item><item>Return for leg weakness, numbness in the groin, bladder or bowel change, fever, night pain, or pain not improving in four to six weeks.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Chronic coronary disease with stable angina (I25.119)</content>. No angina. Cardiac rehabilitation can continue with walking and light cycling; lifting and resistance work paused for one week. [S]<list><item>Continue aspirin, atorvastatin, ezetimibe and metoprolol.</item><item>Rehabilitation team informed.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: 2023 AHA/ACC/ACCP/ASPC/NLA/PCNA Guideline for the Management of Patients With Chronic Coronary Disease (2023); Noninvasive Treatments for Acute, Subacute, and Chronic Low Back Pain: A Clinical Practice Guideline From the American College of Physicians (2017)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Phone check in one week. Coronary disease review in four weeks as booked, or sooner as above. Next scheduled visit 2026-09-21: coronary disease review in cardiac rehabilitation, lipid recheck after ezetimibe, and lip surveillance. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
